<h3>The gist in one sentence</h3>
<p><strong>Instead of running as fast as possible</strong> toward the most powerful systems, the US and China should agree to <strong>deliberately delay the birth of superintelligence until 2040</strong>, make all frontier AI research <strong>public (transparent)</strong> in the meantime, and build a structure in which they can monitor and verify each other. The authors hold that without intervention superintelligence arrives <strong>around 2030</strong>, and they say that <strong>pushing that moment back ten years</strong> until safety infrastructure is in place is &ldquo;the least bad plan I know of.&rdquo;</p>
<blockquote>&ldquo;In AI 2027, we predicted that AI would take over the world or irreversibly concentrate power. In AI 2040: Plan A, we&rsquo;ve laid out our positive vision for what should happen instead.&rdquo;</blockquote>
<p class="scn-sub">— Daniel Kokotajlo (<a href="https://x.com/DKokotajlo/status/2075251618728292464" target="_blank" rel="noopener">X, 2026-07-09</a>)</p>

