<h3>AI 2040: Plan A — Policy and Verification (the Mechanisms That Hold the Deal Together)</h3>
<p>This installment follows the <a href="#!news-20260709-ai-2040-plan-a">AI 2040: Plan A overview</a> and lays out the <strong>policy mechanisms</strong> that keep the scenario rolling. In Plan A's own words, the axis is a <strong>&ldquo;verified deal&rdquo;</strong> — <em>everyone slows down, and everyone watches everyone else</em>. What follows has been cross-checked against the public summary and the reporting on the Supplements.</p>

<h4>① Total Research Transparency</h4>
<p>The core norm is to publish all frontier AI research so that <strong>anyone can see it</strong>. The effects break down into three.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Removes the incentive to hide breakthroughs</strong> — there is no longer any gain in secretly pulling ahead.</li>
    <li><strong>Mutual auditing</strong> — rival labs can audit each other's safety work.</li>
    <li><strong>Blocks covert alignment</strong> — secretly instilling a particular loyalty in a model becomes all but impossible.</li>
</ul>

<h4>② Verification protocol — network taps + partial recomputation</h4>
<p>This is the method for <strong>technically confirming</strong> a claim of &ldquo;being transparent.&rdquo; Much like inference verification:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Network taps</strong> collect evidence of a data center's workloads, and</li>
    <li><strong>partial recomputation</strong> spot-checks whether the packets making up that workload actually match the declared computation.</li>
</ul>
<p>In other words, when the other side says &ldquo;we only computed within the bounds of the deal,&rdquo; this is a structure that <strong>confirms the claim by computation instead of trusting it</strong>.</p>

<h4>③ Relocating data centers — indefensible locations</h4>
<p>The most striking mechanism. R&amp;D data centers are deliberately placed in <strong>locations their own country would find hard to defend</strong>, making it hard to defect from the deal.</p>
<div class="tbl-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Party</th><th>R&amp;D cluster location</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>United States</strong></td><td><strong>Mongolia</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>China</strong></td><td><strong>Canada</strong></td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table></div>
<p>On top of this, <strong>SL5</strong>-level security for model weights and integrity of verification is applied. The original model offers a <strong>concrete numerical trajectory</strong> — for instance, the data centers reciprocally built in Canada and Mongolia running at gigawatt scale at a given point (Canada ~1GW, Mongolia ~2GW) and reaching a certain share (about 15%) of the world's compute by year-end.</p>

<h4>④ Mutually Assured Compute Destruction</h4>
<p>This transplants the Cold War logic of nuclear deterrence (MAD) onto data centers. If the deal breaks down and the other side tries to <strong>seize</strong> a cluster, the side guarding that cluster <strong>destroys its own compute with a &ldquo;scorched-earth&rdquo; mechanism</strong>. The United States places such a mechanism at the Mongolia cluster and China at the Canada cluster, symmetrically. &ldquo;Even if you take it, you can't use it&rdquo; is what drives the gain from defecting to zero.</p>

<h4>⑤ Model spec · third-party oversight</h4>
<p>Alongside the mechanisms above, the model's goals and behavioral norms are codified in a <strong>public model spec</strong>, and compliance with the deal is <strong>overseen by third parties</strong>. Even critics single out these three (research transparency, the model spec, and third-party oversight) as <strong>the strongest part</strong> of Plan A (see Reactions and critiques).</p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">The original's Supplements</span>
    The details of the verification plan and the economic model are provided as an <strong>interactive explorer</strong> in the original's <a href="https://ai-2040.com/supplements" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Supplements</a> (modeling the economy through capital, robots, prices, land, and energy, plus the verification plan in detail). This wiki carries only a <strong>static summary</strong> and leaves reproducing the simulator out of scope.
</div>

<div class="note warn">
    <span class="nh">Facts and estimates</span>
    The <strong>design intent</strong> of these mechanisms (transparency, verification, relocation, compute destruction) is confirmed by public materials. Whether they <strong>could actually work</strong>, however — the political realism of the US and China agreeing to place their nations' core compute in indefensible locations, and the completeness of verification — is heavily contested.
</div>

