<h4>Richard Ngo's three objections (Selective Optimism)</h4>
<p>Ngo has spent the past year as a consultant to the AI Futures Project critiquing and improving this scenario, and at the authors' request his <strong>remaining disagreements ran together with the launch</strong>. The three core points:</p>
<ol>
    <li><strong>The ambiguity of an &ldquo;optimistic forecast&rdquo;</strong> — AI 2040 is neither a pure prediction nor a pure recommendation but an <strong>&ldquo;optimistic forecast,&rdquo;</strong> so while it conveys a great deal of detail about a good future, readers find it hard to sort out <strong>how much of it the authors consider &ldquo;desirable&rdquo; and where that stops</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>The 2040 handover of power</strong> — in the core recommendation, humanity ultimately <strong>hands control of the world over to AI in 2040</strong>. Ngo sees this &ldquo;handover&rdquo; as faster than anyone else's.</li>
    <li><strong>The politics of optimism</strong> — &ldquo;how optimistic to be&rdquo; is a <strong>political choice relative to the default</strong> outlook. The authors can justify this picture on the grounds that &ldquo;slower progress is politically impossible,&rdquo; but in doing so they end up <strong>advocating progress faster than almost anyone else</strong>.</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>&ldquo;I've been working with the AI Futures Project to critique and improve their AI 2040 scenario. Ultimately I still have serious disagreements with it. However, to their credit, the team asked me to write up my criticisms to be shared alongside the scenario itself.&rdquo;</blockquote>
<p class="scn-sub">— Richard Ngo (<a href="https://x.com/RichardMCNgo/status/2075301126921175166" target="_blank" rel="noopener">X</a>)</p>

<h4>The shared issue — if the premises collapse, so does the conclusion</h4>
<p>Where the criticisms overlap is <strong>&ldquo;the realism of the premises.&rdquo;</strong> As Dave Friedman puts it, Plan A sketches a <strong>technically verifiable design</strong>, but whether that is <strong>politically feasible</strong> is another matter. Unless the premises hold — that the US and China trust each other, that they agree to place their nations' core compute in indefensible locations, and that the deal is sustained as a <strong>stable equilibrium</strong> — most of Plan A's conclusions do not follow. Rohit Krishnan likewise points out that &ldquo;individual terms are unclear and underspecified&rdquo; (what counts as AI R&amp;D, what exactly &ldquo;total transparency&rdquo; is).</p>

